Actually, I'm not sure Microsoft actually intended for 
batch files to be used that way, originally.  The ability
to do different things based on an executed program's
exit code may have been included for reasons that would
make sense in a traditional mainframe-style batch 
processing system.  There may not have been any intention
of providing a "shell scripting" functionality.

But people who developed software for the platform 
took about sixteen seconds to realize that this feature
of checking exit codes meant batch files *could* be used 
as essentially a glue programming language.  Anything
the batch file itself can't do directly (e.g., input)
it can just call some third-party utility to do, so
essentially it can do anything.  It's kind of like
Turing equivalence, but applied to I/O rather than
computation.  It takes a little extra doing, but
ultimately a batch file can do more or less anything.  
Nevermind about keyboard input, I used to have batch 
files on my computer that took mouse input, batch
files that did different things depending on what
time of day or day of the week it was, batch files
that called different programs depending on the
result of a computation performed in BASIC, etc.

Actually, most of the stuff that I used to do with
batch files, I wouldn't try to do in a shell script,
on a modern system, because sh is too primitive.  
I mean, it *could* do those things, and people used
to *have* to do those things in sh, which was no
doubt easier than when I did them in batch files;
but it's not the easiest way now.  I have better
options these days.  I could use Perl, for example.
Why would I write eighty lines of hairy sh when 
I can write eight lines of clear, maintainable Perl?

